**How to verify a glossary change — and the command that silently lies.**

The only real test of a row is to regenerate the cleaned VTT from the raw and compare:

```bash
python3 ~/.claude/skills/vtt-spell-pass/apply_replacements.py \
  --vtt <raw>.transcript.vtt --glossary notes/vtt_transcription_corrections.md \
  --output $SCRATCH/regen.vtt
```

⛔ **Do not compare them with `diff A B | grep -c '^[<>]'`.** `diff` on this machine is
wrapped: it prints its own format (`-   1 old` / `+   1 new`), emits **no `<` or `>` lines at
all**, and **exits 0 even when the files differ**. That pipeline therefore reports "identical"
for any pair of files, however different — it reported 0 changes against 21 real ones on
2026-08-28, after being wrongly defended earlier the same day. Use `cmp` for a yes/no, or
compare line-by-line in Python:

```python
strip = lambda p: [re.sub(r'^[^:]*: ', '', l) for l in open(p).read().split('\n')]
for i, (x, y) in enumerate(zip(strip(regen), strip(cleaned)), 1):
    if x != y: print(i, x, y, sep='\n  ')
```

A passing lint plus a green `diff` is **two** pieces of non-evidence stacked on each other.

**Structural issues left alone (fix if `lint_glossary.py --check` starts erroring):**

- **Never put a comma inside the Wrong column unless every fragment is its own
  valid wrong-form.** `find_unknowns.parse_glossary` splits that cell on `,` to
  support multi-variant rows, so a row written as a *phrase* silently becomes
  several one-word rules. Removed 2026-08-20: `| No, Matt | **no, math** |`
  compiled to `No -> no, math` **and** `Matt -> no, math`. The first was saved by
  the `is_self_matching` guard (the canonical contains "no"); the second was live,
  and rewrote any real `Matt` into `no, math`. The row was a one-off table
  utterance, not a name, so it was deleted rather than re-expressed.
- **A case-only correction cannot be expressed in this glossary at all.**
  `apply_replacements.is_self_matching` searches the wrong-form inside the
  canonical *case-insensitively*, so `LionShield Coster -> Lionshield Coster`
  looks non-idempotent and is skipped silently. Dropped that variant from the
  Ch. 47 Lionshield row 2026-08-20; the other two variants are unaffected.
  `LionShield Coster` (no space, capital S) therefore still goes uncorrected —
  the fix belongs in the tool: exempt `wrong.lower() == right.lower()` from the
  guard and match case-sensitively for that one rule.

**One canonical, one row — the rule that keeps dated sections from rotting.**

Fixed 2026-08-24: nine canonicals had rows in two or more sections (`Vukradin` was in
**four**), because each session's glossary loop appends to a new dated section without
checking whether the canonical already has a home. `add_to_glossary.py --section` searches
only within one section, so every append made it worse — a self-worsening bug that
`lint_glossary.py` reports as `split_section`. Twelve duplicate rows were merged away and
the four orphan `| Wrong | Right |` header blocks removed.

**The standing rule:** a dated section (`## Ch. NN additions`) may only hold a row whose
canonical appears **nowhere else in this file**. If the canonical already has a row, add
the new variants to that row and record the provenance as prose in the dated section — the
way the Ch. 47 and Ch. 48 sections now do. Run
`python3 ~/src/mytools/dotfiles/claude/skills/vtt-spell-pass/lint_glossary.py --glossary
notes/vtt_transcription_corrections.md` after every glossary edit; `split_section` warnings
mean the rule was broken again.

Two homes are semantically odd but were left alone, since relocating a row changes where
`add_to_glossary --section` will look for it: **Triboar** (a town) lives in
`## NPCs and creatures`, and **Savras** (a god) in `## Houses / factions`.

